  "conclusion": {
    "hypothesis_rejected": true,
    "summary": "Skewed-t does NOT fix the BTC 1% VaR over-conservatism paradox. All heavy-tail methods (Student-t, HistSim, Skewed-t, Asym-HistSim) produce only 1/731 violations at 1% — all fail Kupiec (p=0.003). Only Normal passes (3/731). At 5%, Student-t, HistSim, and Asym-HistSim all PASS while Normal and Skewed-t FAIL.",
    "root_cause": "The positive skewness exists in raw BTC returns (skew=0.619) but NOT in GJR-GARCH standardized residuals (skew=-0.19). GJR's asymmetric leverage term already absorbs the directional asymmetry. Residual xi=1.036 is essentially 1.0 (symmetric). The real issue is that BTC's df~3.2 creates extremely heavy tails in all directions, making any parametric/nonparametric heavy-tail method too conservative at 1%.",
    "key_findings": [
      "1. Raw BTC returns skew=+0.619 but residual skew=-0.19 after GJR filtering",
      "2. Skewed-t xi=1.036 is essentially symmetric — no meaningful asymmetry left to model",
      "3. Skewed-t df=8.7 vs Student-t df=3.2: Skewed-t uses HIGHER df because xi absorbs some tail behavior, but the 1% quantile is still more conservative (-2.72 vs -2.64)",
      "4. The real issue is GARCH variance over-prediction during the 2023-2024 bull run (mean=0.27%/day), not distributional misspecification",
      "5. For BTC 1% VaR: Normal is the best method (pinball=0.000745, Trinity PASS). Heavy-tail corrections are counterproductive",
      "6. For BTC 5% VaR: Student-t (33/731, pinball=0.00275) and HistSim are optimal. Normal under-covers (22/731)",
      "7. Asym-HistSim produces identical results to HistSim — the negative residual distribution contains the same quantile information",
      "8. This is a REGIME-SPECIFIC finding: 2023-2024 was a strong BTC bull market with low realized downside"
    ],
    "practical_implication": "For BTC VaR: use Normal at 1% (thin-tail asset in bull regimes), Student-t or HistSim at 5%. The 'right' distribution depends on VaR level. No single method passes Trinity at both 1% and 5%.",
    "limitations": [
      "OOS 2023-2024 is a bull market period; bear market results may differ dramatically",
      "BTC trades 24/7 but yfinance daily data misses intraday dynamics",
      "Only tested GJR-GARCH variance model; EGARCH or HAR-RV may behave differently"
    ]
  }
